The user research involved 14 participants, including students, teaching staff, non-teaching staff, and visitors. 

The findings showed that manual queue management remains a challenge at university service points and that users are interested in a digital solution.

## Summary of Findings

64.3% of the respondents were students, making them the primary users of university service points and the main target users of the proposed application.

More than half of the participants visit university service points regularly, showing a strong need for an efficient queue management system.

The Finance Office (64.3%) recorded the highest number of queue experiences, followed by the Food's Mess (57.1%) and the Accommodation Office (35.7%).

Every participant had experienced long queues at least once, with 42.9% experiencing them occasionally and 28.6% experiencing them frequently.

71.4% of participants preferred joining queues remotely using a mobile application instead of waiting in physical queues.

Participants showed a high willingness to use the proposed application, with an average acceptance rating of 4.0 out of 5.


## The most desired features included:
Remote queue registration
Real-time queue position tracking
Estimated waiting time
Push notifications before the user's turn
Support for multiple university service points
Conclusion

The findings indicate that the current manual queuing system causes delays and inconvenience for university users. Most participants supported the introduction of a Virtual Queue Management Mobile Application because it would reduce waiting time, improve transparency, and make accessing university services more convenient.
